# The abstract model - terminology, the value axis, order, the graph, matching, and the
# constructs-in-concretes matrix; each a subdirectory chapter. The facets/type system and
# the metadata vocabulary moved to the toplevel meta chapter (docs/meta).
The logical (abstract) model
description: omnivorous nodes with ordered relations - the terminology and the graph

- >
  The model a yamlover database holds is a **directed** (oriented) **graph**. The graph can have
  cycles (i.e. it is not acyclic).

- >
  The vertices of that graph are called **nodes**. A node may carry a **scalar value** - a string, a
  number, or a boolean. A node carrying no value of its own is taken to hold the special value
  `null`. Nodes are called **omnivorous** because of this.

- >
  Nodes are joined by directed edges called **relations**. A relation runs from a **parent** node to
  a **child** node. Every relation carries an **ordinal**, counted from zero within its parent; a
  relation may additionally carry a string name, its **key**. Keys are unique within one parent.

- >
  Yamlover is a language for writing such graphs down.

- >
  For example, the graph

- !!<*yamlover: $defs: xyflow>
    alice:
      age: 30
      pet: *: whiskers
    bob:
      age: 25
      pet: *: whiskers
    whiskers:
      species: cat
      toys:
        - ball
        - mouse

- >
  can be written as

- !!yo
  alice:
    age: 30
    pet: *: whiskers
  bob:
    age: 25
    pet: *: whiskers
  whiskers:
    species: cat
    toys:
      - ball
      - mouse


- >
  All yamlover concretes are interpreted as (translated to) one logical model: a graph of **omnivorous
  nodes** connected by **ordered relations**.

- *: values
- *: members
- *: order
- *: format
- *: meta
- *: graph
- *: matching
- *: terminology
- *: constructs
